       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_72_v1'
from (values
('127.486.d.1.F',3,'A graph of bacterial growth over time is a way to organize:','["quantitative data", "jokes", "opinions", "names"]'::jsonb,0,'Graphs show counts.','original'),
('127.486.d.1.F',4,'A table comparing colony color, shape, and size on different plates organizes:','["only prices", "qualitative data", "only dates", "only counts"]'::jsonb,1,'Descriptions are qualitative.','original'),
('127.486.d.1.G',3,'A drawing that shows how a virus infects a cell is a:','["guess", "law", "model", "theory only"]'::jsonb,2,'Models represent processes.','original'),
('127.486.d.1.G',4,'A computer simulation of how resistance spreads through a bacterial population is used to represent a:','["budget", "recipe", "banner", "system"]'::jsonb,3,'Models explain systems.','original'),
('127.486.d.1.H',3,'A testable explanation proposed before an experiment is a:','["hypothesis", "theory", "fact", "law"]'::jsonb,0,'A hypothesis is tentative.','original'),
('127.486.d.1.H',4,'A description of what always happens under certain conditions, such as the law of gravity, is a scientific:','["hypothesis", "law", "opinion", "rumor"]'::jsonb,1,'Laws describe patterns.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.A',3,'A model of a bacterium drawn much larger than its real size has a limitation in:','["flavor", "price", "scale", "humor"]'::jsonb,2,'Models are not to scale.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.A',4,'An advantage of a plate-based model of infection is that it can be repeated cheaply, but a limitation is that it lacks a:','["label", "sticker", "lid", "host''s immune response"]'::jsonb,3,'Models omit complexity.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.B',3,'A student sees that colony counts increase every hour until food runs out. This is a growth:','["pattern", "accident", "rumor", "error"]'::jsonb,0,'Patterns are trends.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.B',4,'Average colony count and range are examples of statistical:','["errors", "features", "labels", "guesses"]'::jsonb,1,'Statistics describe data.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.C',3,'If one bacterium divides every 20 minutes, after 60 minutes there are:','["6 bacteria", "3 bacteria", "8 bacteria", "4 bacteria"]'::jsonb,2,'2 x 2 x 2 = 8.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.C',4,'If a culture starts with 100 cells and doubles 4 times, the total is:','["400", "800", "200", "1600"]'::jsonb,3,'100 x 16 = 1600.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.D',3,'Judging whether a new sterilization method worked as intended means evaluating an engineering:','["design", "menu", "tune", "slogan"]'::jsonb,0,'Designs are tested.','original'),
('127.486.d.2.D',4,'Comparing two hand-washing methods by counting remaining bacteria on hands evaluates an experimental:','["recital", "design", "symptom", "recipe"]'::jsonb,1,'Results evaluate designs.','original'),
('127.486.d.3.A',3,'A student explains why mold grew on bread and supports it with moisture readings and models. This explanation is supported by:','["slogans", "gossip", "data", "jokes"]'::jsonb,2,'Evidence supports explanations.','original'),
('127.486.d.3.A',4,'To propose a solution for stopping bacterial spread in a lab, a student would cite results of tests with different:','["pencils", "windows", "chairs", "disinfectants"]'::jsonb,3,'Solutions rely on data.','original'),
('127.486.d.3.B',3,'A group of students presents the results of their culture experiment to the class. This is sharing results through:','["communication", "avoidance", "delay", "secrecy"]'::jsonb,0,'Scientists communicate.','original'),
('127.486.d.3.B',4,'Dividing up tasks like plating, counting, and recording is an example of:','["competition", "collaboration", "conflict", "confusion"]'::jsonb,1,'Teams collaborate.','original'),
('127.486.d.3.C',3,'In a scientific argument about a study, a respectful reply is to ask for the:','["age", "salary", "evidence", "rank"]'::jsonb,2,'Argue with evidence.','original'),
('127.486.d.3.C',4,'When a classmate challenges your conclusion, a good response is to explain how your results support it and be open to:','["no feedback", "winning at all costs", "silence", "other interpretations"]'::jsonb,3,'Open-mindedness is key.','original'),
('127.486.d.4.A',3,'A claim that a cream kills all germs is evaluated by checking whether the study has a:','["control group", "pretty label", "long title", "catchy name"]'::jsonb,0,'Controls are needed.','original'),
('127.486.d.4.A',4,'A critic points out that a bacterial experiment was done only once. This is a weakness in the study''s:','["color", "replication", "font", "title"]'::jsonb,1,'Repeat to confirm.','original'),
('127.486.d.4.B',3,'Louis Pasteur''s experiments with flasks disproved spontaneous generation and supported the idea that microbes come from:','["nothing", "wind only", "other microbes", "rocks"]'::jsonb,2,'Life comes from life.','original'),
('127.486.d.4.B',4,'Robert Koch''s postulates provide steps to prove that a specific microbe causes a specific:','["color", "recipe", "song", "disease"]'::jsonb,3,'Koch linked germs to illness.','original'),
('127.486.d.4.C',3,'A student who wants to become a medical laboratory scientist can learn about the career from a professional:','["organization", "toy shop", "gossip column", "comic strip"]'::jsonb,0,'Associations offer career info.','original'),
('127.486.d.4.C',4,'A museum of medical history is a resource for exploring how scientists discovered:','["new shoes", "microorganisms", "music styles", "road maps"]'::jsonb,1,'Museums teach history.','original'),
('127.486.d.5.A',3,'Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was among the first people to observe microbes using a simple:','["camera", "radio", "microscope", "telescope"]'::jsonb,2,'He saw ''animalcules''.','original'),
('127.486.d.5.A',4,'Joseph Lister reduced deaths after surgery by using carbolic acid to practice:','["distillation", "inflation", "penmanship", "antisepsis"]'::jsonb,3,'Lister pioneered antiseptics.','original'),
('127.486.d.5.B',3,'The agency that tracks and helps control the spread of infectious disease in the United States is the Centers for Disease:','["Control and Prevention", "Counting and Production", "Care and Parking", "Cure and Pricing"]'::jsonb,0,'CDC tracks disease.','original'),
('127.486.d.5.B',4,'The agency that regulates the safety of food, drugs, and medical devices is the:','["Fire Department", "Food and Drug Administration", "Forest Service", "Federal Reserve"]'::jsonb,1,'FDA regulates products.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.A',3,'A tool that asks yes-or-no questions about an organism''s traits to identify it is a:','["pie chart", "bar graph", "dichotomous key", "road map"]'::jsonb,2,'Dichotomous = two choices.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.A',4,'In a dichotomous key, the first question ''Is the cell wall present?'' leads to two:','["colors", "songs", "sizes", "choices"]'::jsonb,3,'Each step has two paths.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.B',3,'In a Gram stain, Gram-positive bacteria appear:','["purple", "green", "pink", "yellow"]'::jsonb,0,'Thick peptidoglycan holds crystal violet.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.B',4,'Gram-negative bacteria stain pink or red because they have a thin wall and an outer:','["scale", "membrane", "shell", "fur"]'::jsonb,1,'Outer membrane differs.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.C',3,'A protein that speeds up a chemical reaction in a microbe is a(n):','["wall", "antibody", "enzyme", "hormone"]'::jsonb,2,'Enzymes are biological catalysts.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.C',4,'Water moving across a bacterial cell membrane because of a concentration difference is called:','["digestion", "meiosis", "mitosis", "osmosis"]'::jsonb,3,'Osmosis moves water.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.D',3,'A lab machine that can make millions of copies of a DNA segment is a polymerase chain reaction:','["thermal cycler", "oven", "fridge", "blender"]'::jsonb,0,'PCR amplifies DNA.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.D',4,'Technology that separates DNA fragments by size using an electric current is gel:','["distillation", "electrophoresis", "incubation", "filtration"]'::jsonb,1,'Gel electrophoresis separates fragments.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.E',3,'A nutrient mixture used to grow microbes in a dish is called:','["gauze", "foil", "agar", "cotton"]'::jsonb,2,'Agar is a growth medium.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.E',4,'A medium that contains blood to show whether bacteria break down red blood cells is a(n):','["sand tray", "paper towel", "water bottle", "blood agar plate"]'::jsonb,3,'Blood agar shows hemolysis.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.F',3,'Microbes that live on the skin or in the gut without causing disease are the normal:','["flora", "toxins", "pathogens", "fauna"]'::jsonb,0,'Normal flora are harmless residents.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.F',4,'E. coli is a normal inhabitant of the human:','["heart", "intestine", "kidney", "eye"]'::jsonb,1,'E. coli lives in the gut.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.G',3,'A microbe that usually does not cause illness but can in a person with a weak immune system is an:','["antibody", "vitamin", "opportunistic pathogen", "enzyme"]'::jsonb,2,'Opportunists take advantage.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.G',4,'Streptococcus pyogenes is the cause of strep throat, a disease classified as:','["viral", "fungal", "parasitic", "bacterial"]'::jsonb,3,'Strep is bacterial.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.H',3,'A visible group of cells grown from a single bacterium on a plate is a:','["colony", "gel", "drop", "membrane"]'::jsonb,0,'Colonies come from one cell.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.H',4,'Describing a colony''s shape, edge, and color is a record of its:','["ownership", "morphology", "history", "pricing"]'::jsonb,1,'Morphology means form.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.I',3,'A report that shows which antibiotics kill an isolated bacterium is a culture and:','["stock report", "sales report", "sensitivity report", "billing record"]'::jsonb,2,'C and S guides therapy.','original'),
('127.486.d.6.I',4,'If a culture and sensitivity report lists a drug as ''resistant,'' then that drug will probably:','["be cheaper", "work best", "be flavored", "not work"]'::jsonb,3,'Resistant means ineffective.','original'),
('127.486.d.7.A',3,'For an infection to spread, a pathogen must leave a reservoir, be transmitted, and enter a susceptible:','["host", "bank", "store", "lab"]'::jsonb,0,'Chain of infection.','original'),
('127.486.d.7.A',4,'Droplets from a sneeze that infect someone nearby are an example of a mode of:','["recovery", "transmission", "sales", "storage"]'::jsonb,1,'Transmission spreads disease.','original'),
('127.486.d.7.B',3,'Tuberculosis is caused by bacteria, ringworm is caused by a fungus, and influenza is caused by a:','["protozoan", "worm", "virus", "bacterium"]'::jsonb,2,'Flu is viral.','original'),
('127.486.d.7.B',4,'Malaria is caused by a single-celled:','["bacterium", "virus", "fungus", "parasite"]'::jsonb,3,'Plasmodium is a protozoan.','original'),
('127.486.d.7.C',3,'White blood cells that engulf germs are part of the body''s defense called the immune:','["response", "menu", "price", "budget"]'::jsonb,0,'Immune cells respond.','original'),
('127.486.d.7.C',4,'Proteins made by the body that attach to specific invaders are:','["enzymes", "antibodies", "fibers", "hormones"]'::jsonb,1,'Antibodies target antigens.','original'),
('127.486.d.7.D',3,'On a plate with antibiotic discs, a clear ring around a disc shows the drug stopped bacteria from growing, called a zone of:','["heat", "growth", "inhibition", "color"]'::jsonb,2,'Zone of inhibition shows effect.','original')
